  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 PM EST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nMild weather will continue through Tuesday with slightly above\nnormal temperatures and dry conditions due to a large surface\nhigh pushing up from the south. Mostly clear skies tonight will\nallow temperatures to fall into the lower 40s. It will not be\nquite as cold as last night since southwest winds will\ncontribute to better boundary layer mixing. This should also\nkeep fog and frost from forming. Winds will diminish\nconsiderable though by Tuesday night to near calm, and\nradiational cooling will drop temperatures into the mid 30s. The\nHRRR is also indicating a large area of dense fog developing\nacross the region on Wednesday morning, which may require\nadvisories.\n\nA deep trough then moves across the Midwest on Wednesday,\nbringing rain to the local area by late afternoon and evening.\nThe accompanying cold front will also bring breezy north winds\ngoing into Thursday. This northerly flow will result in lake\neffect rain showers, and then possibly snow showers on Thursday\nnight and Friday. Right now it is too early to tell exactly how\nmuch snow will fall, but with the recent warm weather, roadways\nshould stay warm enough for little to no accumulation. While it\nwill remain cool on Friday, temps will rise into the low 40s, \ncausing subsequent melting. Showers should taper off on Saturday\nas the large scale low advects farther east of the region and \nhigh pressure builds back in from the west. Dry weather is then \nexpected on Sunday and Monday with gradually warming \ntemperatures. Mondays highs are forecast to be back in the mid \n50s again.",
